          %title Taylors Theorem
          %h1#taylors_theorem Taylor's Theorem
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#preliminary_definitions"} Preliminary definitions
              %li
                %a{:href => "#statements"} Statements
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taylor+polynomial"} Taylor polynomials
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+function"} differentiable function
            approximate it by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial"} polynomial
            functions; the various versions of Taylor’s Theorem describe how good this approximation is. The limiting case of this is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taylor+series"} Taylor series
          %h2#preliminary_definitions Preliminary definitions
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+function"} partial function
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+space"} cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^d
            (or
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^d
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+point"} point
            in the cartesian space, and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            (actually we can allow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ≥
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \geq -1
            also, using
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negative+thinking"} negative thinking
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+function"} differentiable
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              times at
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              %strong Taylor polynomial
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              with order
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              is the unique
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial"} polynomial
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
              variables of degree at most
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              whose derivatives at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              match those of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              up to order
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            It is straightforward (by differentiating a polynomial
            = succeed ")" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                ansatz
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/ansatz"} ?
            = succeed "):" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi T
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %mi k
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mi c
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi n
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo !
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^k_{f,c}(x) = \sum_{n = 0}^k \frac{f^{(n)}(c) (x-c)^n}{n!} .
          %p
            We have written this as if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is a function of one variable; but interpret
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            as a
            %span.newWikiWord
              multi-index
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/multi-index"} ?
            whose length
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            satisfies
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq \ell \leq k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{(n)}
            the mixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+derivative"} partial derivative
            given by that multi-index,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n!
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorial"} factorial
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℓ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo −
                  %mi c
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x-c)^n
            a product of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            factors of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i - c_i
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            is an index appearing in the multi-index
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p In the case of a function of several variables, we can also manage the maximum degrees in the various variables separately, although nobody seems to bother with this.
          %h2#statements Statements
          %p
            There are several different versions of Taylor's Theorem, all stating an extent to which a Taylor polynomial of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
          %p
            Here is the simplest statement, which requires only continuity of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            (which we really only need for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \geq 1
            and not actually necessary for
            = succeed "):" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = -1
          #limit.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous
              at
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo →
                        %mi c
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo −
                        %msubsup
                          %mi T
                          %mrow
                            %mi f
                            %mo ,
                            %mi c
                          %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                            %mi x
                            %mo −
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                          %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim_{x \to c} \frac{{f(x) - T^k_{f,c}(x)}}{{\|x - c\|}^k} = 0 .
          %p
            (The norm
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|\cdot\|}
            can be left out in one variable, or placed in the numerator to handle all components of a vector-valued function at once.)
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{(k)}
            has some continuity, then we get a version of Taylor's Theorem with an integral:
          #integral.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{(k)}
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolutely+continuous+function"} absolutely continuous
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi min
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi max
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\min(x,c),\max(x,c)]
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ,
                        %mi c
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo =
                        %mi a
                      %mi x
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi k
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo −
                        %mi t
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo !
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = T^k_{f,c}(x) + \int_{t=a}^x \frac{f^{(k+1)}(t) (x-t)^{k+1} \,\mathrm{d}t}{(k + 1)!} .
          %p
            (Note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{(k+1)}
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+function"} defined almost everywhere
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrable+function"} Lebesgue integrable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{(k)}
            is absolutely continuous.)
          %p
            This result is not directly very useful is one is using Taylor polynomials to approximate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            where one doesn't know its behaviour, but we have a corollary which can often be used:
          #estimate.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{(k)}
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolutely+continuous+function"} absolutely continuous
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi min
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi max
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\min(x,c),\max(x,c)]
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %msubsup
                        %mi T
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                          %mo ,
                          %mi c
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ≤
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                        %msup
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi x
                            %mo −
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo !
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|f(x) - T^k_{f,c}(x)}| \leq \frac{M {|x-c|}^{k+1}}{(k + 1)!} ,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+bounded+function"} essential upper bound
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{(k+1)}
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi min
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi max
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\min(x,c),\max(x,c)]
          %p
            In many cases, finding a good upper bound of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{(k+1)}
            can be reduced to solving
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{(k+2)}(t) = 0
          %p
            There are also versions that generalize the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mean+value+theorem"} mean value theorem
          #MVT.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_4 Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{(k)}
              is differentiable on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi min
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi max
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\min(x,c),\max(x,c)]
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi min
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi max
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in [\min(x,c),\max(x,c)]
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ,
                        %mi c
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi k
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo −
                        %mi c
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo !
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = T^k_{f,c}(x) + \frac{f^{(k+1)}(t) (x-c)^{k+1}}{(k + 1)!} .
          %p All of these can be generalized in a fairly straightforward way to functions of several variables.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+lemma"} Hadamard lemma
